CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "egy-alexander", set: "ptolemaic-egypt", act: "Conquest", title: "Alexander Takes Egypt", era: "332 – 331 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "Egypt did not fight Alexander. It had been a Persian province for two centuries and it opened the gates.",
  beats: [
    { title: "A liberation, of sorts",
      text: ["To understand why Egypt handed itself over, you have to know what the previous two centuries had felt like from inside it.",
        "**Cambyses** of Persia took the country in 525 BC. Herodotus says he stabbed the **Apis** bull in the thigh and laughed at it — the living god of Memphis, in whose body Ptah was thought to dwell — and that the animal died of the wound. Egyptian inscriptions from the reign say something else: that Cambyses buried an Apis with full honours and paid for the sarcophagus. Both cannot be true, and the disagreement is itself the evidence. Herodotus was writing down what Egyptian priests told him a century later, and by then what they remembered about Persian rule was sacrilege.",
        "Egypt threw the Persians out in 404 BC and stayed free for sixty years under three native dynasties, the last genuinely Egyptian pharaohs the country would ever have. **Nectanebo II** was still building temples when **Artaxerxes III** came back in 343 BC with a large enough army to finish the argument. He fled south into Nubia and vanished from the record. The reconquest was punitive by design: walls pulled down, temples stripped, sacred animals reportedly killed and eaten in front of the priests who kept them.",
        "Egypt had been under **Persian** rule, on and off, since 525 BC, with a bitter interruption of native independence in the fourth century. Persian administration was resented, and the second Persian conquest of 343 BC had been punitive.",
        "So when **Alexander the Great** arrived in 332 BC after taking Tyre and Gaza, the Persian satrap **Mazaces** surrendered without a battle. Alexander sacrificed to **Apis**, the sacred bull whose predecessor a Persian king was said to have insulted, and was recognised as pharaoh. It cost him nothing and bought him a country.",
        "Mazaces had no army to fight with — Persia's field forces had been destroyed at Issus the year before and its king was somewhere in the east, running. But surrender is a choice even when defeat is not, and what Alexander did next made the choice easy to justify.",
        "He went up the Nile to **Memphis** and sacrificed to the Apis. He held Greek games. He gave orders to restore temples the Persians had knocked about. Whether he was formally crowned pharaoh in the Egyptian rite is **Contested** — Egyptian sources treat him as pharaoh and his name appears in a cartouche, while the Greek historians who followed him around do not describe a coronation, which is not the sort of thing they would have left out.",
        "The pattern is worth naming, because the Ptolemies would run it for the next three hundred years. Alexander bought a country with a bull sacrifice and some building work. He could do that only because somebody else had spent two centuries making the country purchasable."],
      key: "Alexander took Egypt without a battle in 332 BC and was accepted as pharaoh after honouring Egyptian gods." },
    { title: "Siwa",
      text: ["**Siwa** is roughly three hundred miles from the coast, most of it desert, and there was no strategic reason whatever to go there. Alexander took a small party and went anyway, in the middle of a war.",
        "The march became a story almost immediately. Arrian, writing from the memoirs of two men who were present, reports that the party lost the track in a sandstorm and was guided to the oasis by animals — and then notes, carefully, that **Ptolemy** said they were talking snakes and **Aristobulus** said they were ravens. He declines to choose. It is one of the cleanest illustrations in ancient history of how quickly a legend forms: within a generation, around men still alive, the story had already grown two incompatible sets of wings.",
        "Alexander then made an arduous desert journey to the oracle of **Amun** at **Siwa**. What the oracle told him was never disclosed; he said only that he had heard what he wished to hear.",
        "What circulated afterwards was that the priest greeted him as son of the god. In Egyptian terms this was routine — pharaohs were sons of Amun by definition. In Greek and Macedonian terms it was explosive, and it fed the divine self-presentation that eventually alienated his own officers. The content of the oracle is **Unknown**; the political use made of it is **Established**.",
        "The oracle at Siwa did not answer in words. The god's image was carried in a barque on the shoulders of priests, and it replied by moving them — forward, back, aside — with the priests interpreting the motion. Whatever Alexander asked, he asked in that setting, and he went into the inner shrine alone.",
        "A later story has the Greek-speaking priest greeting him as *o paidios*, my son, and fumbling it into *pai Dios* — son of Zeus. It is too neat, and it is the kind of thing invented afterwards by people who wanted the divinity to rest on a slip of the tongue.",
        "What can be established is what Alexander did with it. He never confirmed the greeting and never denied it. He began wearing the ram's horns of Amun on his coinage, asked to be buried at Siwa, and by the end was requiring a Persian court prostration from Macedonians who had known him since he was a boy — one of whom, his own court historian Callisthenes, refused, and did not survive the reign."],
      key: "At Siwa Alexander was greeted as son of Amun — routine for a pharaoh, explosive for a Macedonian king." },
    { title: "Alexandria",
      text: ["Before leaving, Alexander marked out a new city on the Mediterranean coast between the sea and Lake Mareotis, at a site with a sheltered harbour and, unusually for the Nile coast, no silting delta.",
        "**Alexandria** was laid out on a Greek grid plan with a causeway to the island of **Pharos**. Alexander never saw it built; he died in Babylon in 323 BC. But he had created the thing that would matter for the next three centuries: a Greek city, facing the Mediterranean rather than up the Nile, from which Greeks would rule Egypt.",
        "There is a story that the surveyors had no chalk and marked the outline in barley meal, and that birds came down and ate the lines before the work was finished; the seers, asked what it meant, said the city would feed the world. It is a good enough story to be suspicious of, and it is at least the right shape — Egypt's grain is exactly what Alexandria would end up exporting.",
        "The plan was handed to **Deinocrates** and built on a grid, with a main street reportedly thirty metres wide running the length of the city and a mile-long causeway, the Heptastadion, out to Pharos, which turned one bay into two harbours. There was already an Egyptian settlement on the site, **Rhakotis**, and it stayed — becoming the Egyptian quarter of a Greek city.",
        "Within a century Alexandria held several hundred thousand people in three communities living under three different legal arrangements: Greeks with citizenship, a large Jewish population with its own governing council, and Egyptians with neither. That arrangement made the city extraordinarily productive and permanently combustible, and both halves of that sentence matter for everything that follows."],
      key: "Alexander founded Alexandria in 331 BC — a Greek city facing the Mediterranean, from which Greeks would rule Egypt." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why did Egypt accept Alexander so readily?", options: ["He promised independence", "It resented two centuries of Persian rule", "He married an Egyptian princess", "He was Egyptian by birth"], correct: 1,
      explain: "He arrived as the alternative to Persia and paid public respect to Egyptian religion, which the Persians had not." },
    { q: "How does the app classify what the oracle at Siwa actually said?", options: ["Established", "Probable", "Contested", "Unknown"], correct: 3,
      explain: "Alexander never disclosed it; only the political use made of it can be established." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "egy-ptolemy1", set: "ptolemaic-egypt", act: "Dynasty", title: "Ptolemy I Soter and the Body", era: "323 – 282 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "How one of Alexander's bodyguards turned a provincial governorship into a three-hundred-year dynasty.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Choosing the safe province",
      text: ["The division at Babylon in 323 BC was supposed to be temporary: a regency holding the empire together for Alexander's unborn son and his half-brother. Nobody who mattered believed it. Ptolemy is reported to have argued at that meeting for dividing the empire outright rather than pretending — which, if true, means he understood the situation about ten years before most of his colleagues did.",
        "When Alexander died in 323 BC his generals — the **Diadochi**, the Successors — divided the empire and then fought over it for forty years. Most chased the whole thing. **Ptolemy son of Lagus**, a Macedonian noble and one of Alexander's bodyguards, asked for Egypt.",
        "It was the shrewdest choice available. Egypt was rich, agriculturally self-sufficient, and defensible: desert on two sides, sea on the third, and a single practicable land approach at Sinai. While rivals bled each other across Asia, Ptolemy consolidated. He declared himself king in 305 BC and took the title **Soter**, Saviour.",
        "He moved on Egypt like a man taking possession rather than a post. **Cleomenes**, the finance officer Alexander had left in charge of the treasury, was tried and executed, and the eight thousand talents he had accumulated by manipulating the grain trade became Ptolemy's working capital. Cyrene was annexed. The frontier at Sinai was fortified.",
        "Then he spent forty years not overreaching. When **Antigonus the One-Eyed** made his bid for the whole empire, Ptolemy fought him at arm's length and was content to lose ground he could get back — his fleet was smashed at Salamis off Cyprus in 306 BC by Antigonus's son Demetrius, a defeat so complete it cost him Cyprus for a decade. The following year Demetrius besieged **Rhodes**, and Ptolemy's support helped the city hold out. The grateful Rhodians gave him the cult title **Soter**, Saviour, and it stuck to him and to his line.",
        "Every other Successor chased the whole inheritance. Antigonus died on the field at Ipsus at eighty. Perdiccas, Seleucus, Lysimachus and Ptolemy Keraunos were all murdered or killed in battle. Ptolemy took the one province that could feed itself and be defended at a single gate, and died in bed at eighty-four, having already made his son co-ruler so that the succession happened while he was alive to supervise it."],
      key: "Ptolemy chose Egypt for its wealth and defensibility, declared himself king in 305 BC, and took the title Soter." },
    { title: "Hijacking the funeral",
      text: ["The catafalque had taken two years to build and was a machine as much as a hearse. Diodorus describes a gold vaulted canopy on Ionic columns, a Doric frieze, bells to warn of its approach, a chamber with a gold sarcophagus inside it, and shock-absorbing suspension so the body would not be jolted on rough ground — with a road crew of engineers and labourers travelling ahead of it to make the roads passable. It moved at walking pace, drawn by sixty-four mules, and crowds came out to see it pass.",
        "Alexander's embalmed body was being escorted to Macedon in an extraordinary gold funerary carriage. Ptolemy intercepted the cortege and diverted it to Egypt — first to Memphis, later to a tomb in Alexandria.",
        "This was not sentiment. In a world where legitimacy flowed from Alexander, possession of his corpse and his tomb was a political asset no rival could match, and it made Alexandria a pilgrimage destination for centuries. **Perdiccas**, the regent, invaded Egypt to get the body back, failed at a Nile crossing, and was murdered by his own officers. Ptolemy's position was made.",
        "Perdiccas came after it with the royal army and got as far as the Nile opposite Memphis. He tried to force a crossing at a ford his scouts had misjudged; the current deepened as the men churned the bed, and Diodorus says over a thousand were swept away or taken by crocodiles while the rest watched from the bank. His officers killed him in his tent within days.",
        "Ptolemy was offered the regency of the whole empire on the spot, and refused it. He had already worked out that being the man who held Alexander was worth more than being the man who held Alexander's job.",
        "He also wrote the history. Ptolemy's memoir of the campaigns is lost, but it was the main source for Arrian four centuries later, which means our steadiest account of Alexander comes from a man with a professional interest in how the story ended up being told."],
      key: "Ptolemy hijacked Alexander's funeral cortege to Egypt, gaining a legitimacy asset no rival could match." },
    { title: "Two crowns, one king",
      text: ["Ptolemy governed as two people at once. To Greeks and Macedonians he was a Hellenistic king ruling through a Greek-speaking bureaucracy from Alexandria. To Egyptians he was **pharaoh**, crowned by Egyptian rites, funding temple building at Edfu and elsewhere, and depicted in traditional form on temple walls.",
        "He also promoted a deliberately hybrid god, **Serapis** — Egyptian Osiris-Apis given a Greek appearance and a Greek-style temple, the **Serapeum**. Whether Serapis was an invention of Ptolemy's court or an existing Memphite cult that was promoted and reshaped is **Contested**; what is clear is the intent, which was to give a Greek ruling class and an Egyptian population a god they could share. The two-crown system held for three centuries, and it never quite merged.",
        "Underneath the theology was a machine. Greek and Macedonian soldiers were settled on land grants as **cleruchs** and became a standing military class with a stake in the regime. Administration ran in Greek through a **dioiketes**, a chief finance minister with a reach no pharaoh's vizier had ever had: the crown took a share of the grain harvest, and ran outright monopolies in vegetable oil, textiles, papyrus, salt and banking, with the whole system recorded on papyrus in triplicate. More Ptolemaic paperwork survives than from any other ancient state, because Egypt is dry.",
        "What that machine did not do was let Egyptians into it. The senior posts were Greek, the courts were separate, the language of power was Greek, and for nearly three hundred years no Ptolemaic monarch bothered to learn the language of the country they were crowned in. The first who did was the last of them."],
      key: "Ptolemy ruled as Greek king and Egyptian pharaoh simultaneously and promoted Serapis as a shared Greek-Egyptian god." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why was Egypt the shrewdest of the Successor provinces?", options: ["It was closest to Macedon", "It was rich, self-sufficient and defensible", "It had the largest army", "It was already Greek-speaking"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Deserts and sea meant one practicable land approach, so Ptolemy could consolidate while rivals fought." },
    { q: "What was the political value of Alexander's body?", options: ["Religious relics for temples", "Legitimacy no rival Successor could match", "A bargaining chip with Persia", "Nothing — it was sentiment"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Possession of the corpse and tomb anchored Ptolemaic legitimacy and made Alexandria a destination." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "egy-ptolemy2", set: "ptolemaic-egypt", act: "Dynasty", title: "The Library and the Lighthouse", era: "282 – 246 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "Ptolemy II spent Egypt's grain revenue on making Alexandria the intellectual capital of the world. It worked.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Philadelphus",
      text: ["**Arsinoe II** had already lived two lifetimes before she married her brother. At about sixteen she was married to **Lysimachus**, one of the older Successors, and bore him three sons; when he died at Corupedium she found herself in a kingdom being carved up, and married her own half-brother **Ptolemy Keraunos** to secure her boys' position. He murdered two of them shortly afterwards, in front of her, and took Macedon. She escaped to Samothrace, and then to Egypt, and then to the throne of it.",
        "**Ptolemy II** married his full sister **Arsinoe II**, which earned them both the epithet **Philadelphus**, sibling-loving. To Greeks it was scandalous; to Egyptians, divine siblings marrying was a familiar royal pattern going back to Osiris and Isis.",
        "It became dynastic policy. Sibling marriage concentrated the succession, avoided powerful in-laws, and reinforced the family's divine status — and, over ten generations, produced a court where the nearest rival to the throne was always the person across the dinner table. Arsinoe was no ornament: she was politically formidable, deified in her lifetime, and had cities named for her.",
        "The scale of the court she presided over is best caught by the **Grand Procession** that Ptolemy II staged at Alexandria, described by an eyewitness whose account survives at second hand. It took a day to pass. There were elephants in fours drawing chariots, camels carrying spices by the hundredweight, ostriches in harness, a rhinoceros, giraffes, a python thirty feet long, and hunting dogs by the two thousand; four hundred cartloads of silver plate and eight hundred of gold; and a mechanical statue of Nysa that stood up on its own, poured a libation of milk, and sat down again.",
        "None of it was decoration. A Hellenistic monarchy had no ancient right to rule and no institution behind it, so it advertised instead — wealth, reach, control of Africa and Asia, and a technology nobody else had. The procession was a budget statement with animals in it."],
      key: "Ptolemy II married his sister Arsinoe II, establishing sibling marriage as Ptolemaic dynastic policy." },
    { title: "The Museum and the Great Library",
      text: ["The idea seems to have come with **Demetrius of Phalerum**, an Athenian philosopher-politician who had governed Athens for Macedon, been thrown out, and arrived in Alexandria with a scheme and no city. Aristotle's school had collected books to work from. Demetrius proposed collecting all of them.",
        "The **Mouseion** — a shrine to the Muses and effectively a state-funded research institute — gave scholars salaries, board and freedom from teaching. Attached to it was the **Great Library**, whose stated ambition was to hold a copy of every book in the world.",
        "The acquisition methods were aggressive: ships docking at Alexandria were searched and their books copied, with the copies sometimes returned and the originals kept. The results justified it. **Euclid** systematised geometry here. **Eratosthenes** calculated the Earth's circumference to within a few per cent using shadow angles. **Aristarchus** proposed a sun-centred cosmos eighteen centuries before Copernicus. The Hebrew scriptures were translated into Greek as the **Septuagint**. Holdings are usually estimated in the hundreds of thousands of scrolls, though ancient figures vary wildly and are **Contested**.",
        "The most revealing acquisition was a fraud. Ptolemy III asked Athens to lend him the official state copies of Aeschylus, Sophocles and Euripides — the master texts against which every performance was checked — and put down fifteen talents as a deposit, an enormous sum. He had them copied, sent the copies back, kept the originals, and forfeited the money. Athens had been outbid for its own literature.",
        "The point of all that hoarding was not to own scrolls; it was that the same text existed in Alexandria in twenty conflicting versions, and somebody had to decide which words Homer actually wrote. **Zenodotus**, **Aristophanes of Byzantium** and **Aristarchus** worked out how: compare the copies, mark suspect lines, argue from the author's own usage, and record the reasoning. **Callimachus** catalogued the holdings in the *Pinakes*, a hundred and twenty scrolls of what was in the building and who wrote it.",
        "That is the Library's real bequest. Not the shelves, which burned or rotted or were dispersed by degrees, but the idea that a text has a correct version and that finding it is work — the discipline every printed edition you have ever read still depends on."],
      key: "The Museum and Great Library made Alexandria the ancient world's research capital, producing Euclid and Eratosthenes." },
    { title: "Pharos",
      text: ["On the island at the harbour mouth rose the **Pharos lighthouse**, begun under Ptolemy I and completed under Ptolemy II. Estimates put it around 100 metres tall — among the tallest structures on Earth for centuries, and one of the Seven Wonders.",
        "A fire burned at the top, with a mirror or polished reflector said to project the light far out to sea; the more extravagant claims about burning enemy ships are later embellishment. It stood for over 1,500 years before earthquakes brought it down, and blocks from it have been recovered from the harbour floor. Its name became the word for lighthouse in several languages.",
        "Library and lighthouse were the same policy in two forms: Egypt's agricultural surplus converted into prestige, and prestige into the pull that made Alexandria the largest and richest city in the Mediterranean world.",
        "It was built in three stages — a square base, an octagonal middle, a cylindrical lantern — and the base was pierced by a spiral ramp wide enough for pack animals, because a fire that size needs fuel carried up it every day in a country with almost no wood.",
        "Lucian tells a story about its architect, **Sostratus of Cnidus**, who is said to have carved his own name into the stone, plastered over it, and inscribed the king's name in the plaster, so that the flattery would weather away in a century or two and the truth underneath would outlast it. It is probably too good to be true, and it has been repeated for two thousand years, which is its own kind of monument.",
        "Earthquakes took the top in 956 and the rest in 1303 and 1323, and in 1480 the Mamluk sultan Qaitbay built a fort on the foundations out of the fallen blocks, where it still stands. In the 1990s divers surveying the harbour floor brought up columns, sphinxes and masonry blocks weighing dozens of tonnes, and the lighthouse turned out to have been there the whole time, just underwater."],
      key: "The Pharos lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders, stood over 1,500 years and gave several languages their word for lighthouse." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What did Eratosthenes achieve at Alexandria?", options: ["Invented the alphabet", "Calculated the Earth's circumference to within a few per cent", "Built the Pharos", "Translated the Septuagint"], correct: 1,
      explain: "He used the difference in shadow angles between two Egyptian cities at the same moment — a landmark piece of measurement." },
    { q: "Why did the Ptolemies adopt sibling marriage?", options: ["Greek religious law required it", "It concentrated succession, avoided in-laws and reinforced divine status", "It was forced on them by Rome", "To ally with Persia"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It was dynastic engineering that also mapped onto an existing Egyptian royal and divine pattern." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "egy-syrian-wars", set: "ptolemaic-egypt", act: "Dynasty", title: "The Syrian Wars", era: "274 – 168 BC", minutes: 6,
  intro: "Six wars against the Seleucids for the same strip of coast. The detail belongs in the Wars tab; the consequences belong here.",
  beats: [
    { title: "What they were fighting over",
      text: ["**Coele-Syria** — roughly the Levantine coast and its hinterland — sat between Ptolemaic Egypt and the **Seleucid Empire**. Both claimed it on the strength of rival arrangements made in the chaos after Alexander's death.",
        "It mattered for timber for shipbuilding, which Egypt lacks, for the land approach to the Nile Delta, and for control of the caravan and incense routes. Six **Syrian Wars** were fought over it between 274 and 168 BC. A full entry for them will appear under Wars once the Seleucid Empire has its own Set — this app will not run a war entry with only one side studied.",
        "The fighting itself was mostly conventional and mostly indecisive: armies marched down the coast road, took the fortified ports, and gave them back at the peace. What ran alongside it was more interesting. The Second Syrian War ended with a marriage — Antiochus II set aside his wife Laodice to marry Ptolemy II's daughter Berenice, with an enormous dowry — and when he died, Laodice is said to have had Berenice and her infant son killed, which started the Third Syrian War. Ptolemaic diplomacy repeatedly tried to buy Syria with daughters, and repeatedly got a war back with interest."],
      key: "The Syrian Wars were fought over Coele-Syria for timber, trade routes and the land approach to Egypt." },
    { title: "Raphia, and the consequence nobody planned",
      text: ["The Ptolemaic army had always been a Greek and Macedonian army, topped up with mercenaries. That was policy: an Egyptian phalanx would have been cheaper and closer to hand, and the dynasty had spent a century deciding it did not want one.",
        "In 217 BC it ran out of alternatives. Antiochus III was coming down the coast with an army the mercenary market could not match, so the government did the thing it had avoided for a hundred years: it conscripted about twenty thousand native Egyptians, drilled them in the Macedonian manner, and put them in the line.",
        "The turning point came at **Raphia** in 217 BC, where **Ptolemy IV** beat **Antiochus III**. To field a large enough army, the Ptolemies had for the first time armed and trained a substantial force of **native Egyptian** infantry in the Macedonian manner.",
        "They won — and then discovered they had created a constituency. Egyptians who had fought for the dynasty expected standing from it. Within a year, revolt broke out in the south, and for roughly twenty years Upper Egypt was ruled by native pharaohs at Thebes, independent of Alexandria.",
        "Polybius, who understood exactly what had happened, says the Egyptians came out of Raphia unwilling to be commanded any longer and looking for a leader — and found several. The revolt that began in 207 BC put Upper Egypt under two successive native pharaohs, **Horwennefer** and **Ankhwennefer**, ruling from Thebes and recognised by the southern temples for twenty years. Alexandria could not spare the troops to end it until 186 BC.",
        "The dynasty had discovered the cost of its own founding compromise. For a century it had kept Egyptians out of the army precisely so they would not learn what they could do, and the first time it needed them it taught them."],
      key: "Arming native Egyptians won Raphia in 217 BC and triggered a twenty-year secession of Upper Egypt under native pharaohs." },
    { title: "The slide",
      text: ["The Fifth Syrian War went badly. **Antiochus III** took Coele-Syria for good around 200 BC, stripping Egypt of its overseas possessions in the Levant.",
        "From here the Ptolemaic pattern is set: military decline abroad, revolt and priestly bargaining at home, and an increasing habit of asking Rome to adjudicate. Every appeal solved an immediate problem and deepened a permanent dependency.",
        "The Fifth Syrian War is also where Egypt's overseas position ends. Antiochus took the Levant at **Panium** in 200 BC and kept it, and the Ptolemaic empire — Cyprus, the Aegean islands, the coastal cities, the protectorate in the Cyclades — contracted over the following decades to Egypt itself and Cyprus.",
        "Antiochus then offered a peace sealed by his daughter **Cleopatra Syra**, married to the boy king Ptolemy V, which is where the name enters the Egyptian dynasty. She was sent as an agent of Seleucid influence and became, as far as anyone can tell, a genuinely effective Egyptian queen — the first of seven Cleopatras, and the reason the last one had the name at all."],
      key: "Egypt lost Coele-Syria around 200 BC and began appealing to Rome to settle its disputes." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why does the app not yet give the Syrian Wars a full war entry?", options: ["The sources are lost", "The Seleucid Empire does not yet have a Set, so only one side has been studied", "They were not important", "They were fought at sea"], correct: 1,
      explain: "War entries require both sides' histories up to the war — that rule applies even when it is inconvenient." },
    { q: "What was the unintended consequence of the victory at Raphia?", options: ["Bankruptcy", "Native Egyptian troops became a political constituency and Upper Egypt seceded", "Loss of the fleet", "Roman occupation"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Arming a population changes its relationship to the state that armed it — here, within a year." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "egy-canopus-rosetta", set: "ptolemaic-egypt", act: "Dynasty", title: "Canopus and the Rosetta Stone", era: "238 – 196 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "Two priestly decrees, carved in three scripts — and one of them eventually gave the world back the ability to read Egyptian.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The Decree of Canopus, 238 BC",
      text: ["A synod was a negotiation. Every few years the priests of Egypt's temples assembled, and what came out was a decree in the priests' own voice, honouring the king and listing what he had done for them — which is to say, listing the price.",
        "Under **Ptolemy III**, a synod of Egyptian priests at **Canopus** issued a decree honouring the king, recording famine relief through imported grain, the return of religious statues taken by Persia, and a new cult for the king's dead daughter Berenice.",
        "It also attempted a **leap year** — an extra day every fourth year to stop the 365-day Egyptian calendar drifting against the seasons. The Egyptian priesthood largely ignored the reform, but the idea reached Rome: it is essentially the mechanism Caesar adopted for the Julian calendar two centuries later.",
        "The decree was inscribed in **hieroglyphs, demotic and Greek** — the standard format for these priestly bargains, because each script addressed a different audience.",
        "The leap-year clause is worth dwelling on, because it shows how good the astronomy already was. The Egyptian civil year was exactly 365 days with no correction, so it slipped against the sun by a day every four years and made a full circuit of the seasons every 1,460 — a calendar in which midsummer eventually falls in winter. Canopus proposed a sixth epagomenal day every fourth year to stop the drift, and the priests, whose festivals and temple revenues were tied to the old reckoning, simply did not adopt it.",
        "The idea did not die. Two centuries later, an Alexandrian astronomer named Sosigenes advised **Julius Caesar** on calendar reform, and the mechanism Caesar imposed on Rome — a leap day every fourth year — is the one the Egyptian priesthood had turned down. It is still, with one Renaissance adjustment, the calendar on your wall."],
      key: "The Decree of Canopus (238 BC) attempted a leap year and was inscribed in hieroglyphs, demotic and Greek." },
    { title: "The Rosetta Stone, 196 BC",
      text: ["The same mechanism produced the **Rosetta Stone** under **Ptolemy V**, a boy king whose reign was beset by the Upper Egyptian secession and Seleucid attack. The decree records the priests of Memphis confirming his royal cult in exchange for tax remissions, temple endowments and an amnesty.",
        "Its content is routine. Its form is not: the same text in hieroglyphic, demotic and Greek. Rediscovered by French soldiers in 1799 and ceded to Britain in 1801, it gave scholars a known language beside an unknown script. **Thomas Young** established that cartouches spelled royal names phonetically; **Jean-François Champollion** cracked the system in 1822, proving hieroglyphs mixed sound-signs and meaning-signs rather than being purely symbolic.",
        "Fourteen centuries of Egyptian history became readable again because a weak king needed to buy the support of his priests.",
        "The stone itself is a fragment: the top is broken off, so of the fourteen surviving lines of hieroglyphs perhaps half the original text is missing, and the demotic section is the only one that is nearly complete. It was found in 1799 in the wall of a fort at Rashid, where it had been reused as building material — a decree meant to stand in every major temple in Egypt, ending up as rubble in a rampart.",
        "Decipherment took twenty-three years and was a contest. **Thomas Young** got the crucial part first: that the cartouches spelled royal names phonetically, and that demotic and hieroglyphic were related scripts rather than separate systems. **Champollion**, who had taught himself Coptic — the last descendant of the Egyptian language, still used in church liturgy — saw what Young could not: that the phonetic signs were not a special case for foreign names but the ordinary machinery of the script, and that the language underneath was one he already knew."],
      key: "The Rosetta Stone's trilingual decree let Champollion decipher hieroglyphs in 1822, reopening 3,000 years of Egyptian writing." },
    { title: "What the decrees reveal",
      text: ["Read as politics rather than piety, these inscriptions show the balance of power. A dynasty confident of its position does not need to purchase the priesthood's blessing in three scripts.",
        "Each decree records concessions: tax exemptions, temple funding, amnesties for rebels. The Greek ruling class was steadily conceding ground to Egyptian institutions in exchange for legitimacy — and the temples, which held enormous land and grain, were the only network reaching the whole country.",
        "There is a second reading available, and it is worth holding alongside the first. The priesthoods were not simply extracting concessions from a weak monarchy; they were also the institution through which a Greek dynasty could be made intelligible to the country it ruled. A decree in three scripts is a bargain, but it is also a translation — and the direction of translation was towards Egypt, not away from it.",
        "By the last century of the dynasty the temples were building on a scale not seen since the New Kingdom, at Edfu, Dendera, Kom Ombo and Philae, in a fully traditional style, funded by Greek kings who could not read the walls. Most of what a visitor to Egypt photographs today as ancient temple architecture was put up by the Ptolemies."],
      key: "The trilingual decrees record the Ptolemies buying priestly legitimacy with tax and temple concessions." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What made the Rosetta Stone decisive for decipherment?", options: ["It was the oldest Egyptian text", "It carried the same text in hieroglyphs, demotic and Greek", "It was written by Cleopatra", "It listed all Egyptian kings"], correct: 1,
      explain: "A known language beside an unknown script is what makes decipherment tractable." },
    { q: "What do the priestly decrees reveal about Ptolemaic power?", options: ["It was absolute", "The dynasty was buying legitimacy from Egyptian temples with concessions", "The priests had been abolished", "Greek was no longer spoken"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The concessions recorded in the text are the real content — a strong dynasty would not need to make them." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "egy-rome-protector", set: "ptolemaic-egypt", act: "Decline", title: "A Circle in the Sand", era: "168 – 51 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "The moment Egypt stopped being a great power is precisely datable, and it did not involve a battle.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The Day of Eleusis, 168 BC",
      text: ["**Antiochus IV** had already taken Egypt once, in 170 BC, and held the boy king Ptolemy VI as a guest who could not leave. Alexandria answered by crowning his younger brother instead, so Antiochus withdrew, leaving two Ptolemies to fight each other — and came back in 168 BC when they made it up.",
        "This time there was nothing in his way. He took Memphis, was crowned pharaoh by some accounts, and marched on Alexandria. Egypt, with no army worth the name, sent to Rome.",
        "**Antiochus IV** of the Seleucid Empire invaded Egypt, defeated its forces and was approaching Alexandria in 168 BC. Egypt appealed to Rome.",
        "Rome sent one envoy, **Gaius Popillius Laenas**, with no army. He handed Antiochus the Senate's demand to withdraw. Antiochus said he would consult his advisors. Popillius drew a circle in the sand around the king's feet and told him to answer before he stepped out of it.",
        "Antiochus withdrew. Rome had removed a Seleucid army from Egypt with a stick and a sentence — because Antiochus had seen what Rome did to Macedon at Pydna that same year. Egypt survived as a state and ceased, from that day, to be an independent great power.",
        "Popillius and Antiochus knew each other; they had been friends in Rome when Antiochus was a hostage there. The envoy declined to take his hand until the business was settled, and the business was a tablet.",
        "What made the circle work was not in the sand. Twenty-two days earlier, at **Pydna** in Macedon, a Roman army had destroyed the phalanx of Perseus and ended the kingdom of Alexander's homeland — and the news had reached Alexandria. Antiochus had just been shown, at no cost to himself, what refusing Rome now looked like.",
        "Egypt kept its king, its borders and its revenue, and lost the thing that is harder to see going. From 168 BC the question of who ruled Egypt had a Roman answer available, and everyone in the eastern Mediterranean knew it — including every future claimant to the Egyptian throne, who from now on would campaign in Rome before campaigning in Egypt."],
      key: "In 168 BC one Roman envoy forced a Seleucid army out of Egypt by drawing a circle in the sand." },
    { title: "The wills",
      text: ["Rome's leverage then became legal. In 155 BC **Ptolemy VIII** made a will bequeathing Cyrene to Rome should he die without heirs — an insurance policy against assassination, since killing him would now hand territory to Rome.",
        "The device spread. **Ptolemy XI** ruled for nineteen days in 80 BC before being lynched for murdering his co-ruler, and a will attributed to him left the whole kingdom to Rome. Whether that will was genuine is **Contested** and was doubted in antiquity; Cicero discusses the claim without settling it. Rome did not enforce it — but never renounced it either. Every subsequent Ptolemy ruled knowing Rome held a claim it could activate at will.",
        "It is worth being clear about how odd this instrument was. A will leaving a kingdom to a foreign republic was not a Ptolemaic invention — Attalus III of Pergamon did it in 133 BC and Rome took the province — and it worked as protection precisely because it was credible. Murdering the king would not put your candidate on the throne; it would put Rome there.",
        "The cost was that the dynasty had written its own extinction and deposited it with the beneficiary. Rome could produce the document whenever producing it was convenient, and the mere possibility shaped every Ptolemaic decision for the next fifty years. Nothing had been conquered. A kingdom had simply signed a contingent transfer of itself and then lived inside the contingency."],
      key: "Ptolemy XI's disputed will left Egypt to Rome; Rome never enforced it and never renounced it." },
    { title: "Ptolemy XII buys his throne",
      text: ["**Ptolemy XII** was illegitimate, which in a dynasty this depleted was no longer disqualifying, and he was nicknamed **Auletes**, the flute-player, by subjects who thought a king should not perform in public. He spent his reign in the one activity that actually mattered: keeping Rome from cashing the will.",
        "**Ptolemy XII Auletes**, Cleopatra's father, made the dependency explicit. He purchased Roman recognition with enormous bribes — reportedly around 6,000 talents to Caesar and Pompey, roughly a year of Egypt's entire revenue — borrowed from the Roman financier **Rabirius Postumus**.",
        "The taxes needed to service it made him hated. Driven out in 58 BC, he was restored in 55 BC by a Roman army under **Aulus Gabinius**, which then stayed in Egypt as a garrison. Rabirius was installed as finance minister to collect on his own loan.",
        "So when Ptolemy XII died in 51 BC, leaving the kingdom to his teenage daughter **Cleopatra VII** and her younger brother **Ptolemy XIII**, with Rome named as guarantor of the will, the position was this: a rich kingdom, a disputed throne, a Roman debt, Roman soldiers in the country, and a Roman claim to the whole thing.",
        "The middle of it is worth following, because it is the moment Egypt stops being a state that pays Rome and becomes a state Rome operates. In 58 BC Rome annexed **Cyprus**, Ptolemaic territory ruled by Auletes's own brother, who took poison rather than accept the demotion to a temple priesthood he was offered. Auletes said nothing — he could not afford to — and Alexandria threw him out for it.",
        "He went to Rome and lobbied. Alexandria sent a delegation of a hundred leading citizens to argue the other side; most of them were killed or bought off before they could be heard, and the affair was notorious enough that Cicero was still referring to it years later. The restoration cost ten thousand talents, paid to Gabinius, who marched from Syria without authorisation to collect it.",
        "Rabirius took over the treasury to recover his own loan, ran it so hard that he had to flee the country, and was prosecuted in Rome. The Gabinian troops stayed, married locally, and within a few years were a Roman garrison with Egyptian loyalties that Rome could not rely on either."],
      key: "Ptolemy XII bought Roman backing with ruinous loans and was restored by a Roman army that then garrisoned Egypt." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What did Popillius Laenas bring to Egypt in 168 BC?", options: ["Four legions", "A fleet", "A single Senate demand and no army", "Grain relief"], correct: 2,
      explain: "Rome's reputation after Pydna did the work; the circle in the sand simply removed the option of delay." },
    { q: "What was Egypt's position when Ptolemy XII died in 51 BC?", options: ["Independent and prosperous", "A Roman province", "Formally independent but garrisoned, indebted and subject to a Roman claim", "Allied with Parthia"], correct: 2,
      explain: "Every lever of independence had been pledged, borrowed against or conceded before Cleopatra inherited." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "egy-cleopatra-caesar", set: "ptolemaic-egypt", act: "The Last Queen", title: "Cleopatra and Caesar", era: "51 – 44 BC", minutes: 10,
  intro: "The most famous political partnership in ancient history, and one of the most consistently misreported.",
  beats: [
    { title: "A queen at eighteen",
      text: ["**Cleopatra VII** inherited in 51 BC as co-ruler with her brother **Ptolemy XIII**, aged about ten, and married to him by dynastic custom. Real power sat with his court faction: the eunuch **Pothinus**, the general **Achillas**, and the rhetorician **Theodotus**.",
        "She was not a passive figure. She dropped her brother's name from official documents and put her own image alone on coinage. **Plutarch** records that she learned **Egyptian**, apparently the first of her line to bother, and spoke numerous languages without interpreters — classified **Probable**, on a good source. She presented herself as the goddess **Isis** to Egyptian subjects. By 49 BC the court had pushed her out of Alexandria; she was camped near the eastern frontier raising an army.",
        "The Egypt she inherited was in trouble beyond the politics. The Nile flood failed two years running, and a royal decree of 50 BC survives ordering grain shipped to Alexandria and nowhere else, on pain of death — a government feeding its capital and letting the country manage. Her name appears on that decree in a single word added in another hand at the bottom, apparently her own: *ginesthoi* — let it be done.",
        "When the court moved against her she did not negotiate from inside it. She left for Syria, raised troops on her own credit, and came back to the eastern frontier with an army, which is where a Roman civil war found her: an eighteen-year-old with no capital, no treasury and no throne, camped on the border of her own country waiting for an opening."],
      key: "Cleopatra inherited at eighteen, was pushed out by her brother's court faction by 49 BC, and raised an army to return." },
    { title: "A head in a basket",
      text: ["Rome's civil war then arrived. **Pompey**, beaten at Pharsalus, sailed to Egypt expecting refuge from a dynasty his family had patronised. Ptolemy XIII's advisors calculated that a dead Pompey would please the winner and a live one would be a liability. Theodotus reportedly settled the debate by observing that dead men do not bite.",
        "Pompey was stabbed as he stepped ashore, in sight of his wife. When **Caesar** arrived days later, he was presented with the head. Every source agrees he was appalled, and Plutarch has him weeping. Part of that was genuine — Pompey had been his son-in-law — and part was Roman: foreign courtiers had presumed to execute a Roman consul and triumphator. It was also the worst possible opening move, because it removed the one man who could have limited Caesar's involvement in Egypt.",
        "His body was left on the sand. An old freedman named Philip, who had come ashore with him, gathered driftwood from the beach to burn it, and a passing veteran who had served under Pompey thirty years earlier stopped to help him do it. Caesar had the ashes sent to Pompey's widow."],
      key: "Ptolemy XIII's court murdered Pompey to please Caesar; it appalled him and drew him directly into Egypt's civil war." },
    { title: "Getting into the room",
      text: ["Caesar had come ashore with a bodyguard and his **lictors** — the axe-bearing attendants of a Roman consul — and walked into Alexandria in state, which read to the city exactly as it was meant to: a Roman magistrate exercising jurisdiction in a foreign capital. There was rioting within days and some of his soldiers were killed in the streets. He then settled into the palace quarter and refused to leave until the succession was arranged, which was the moment Cleopatra needed and her brother's court could not prevent.",
        "Caesar occupied the palace quarter, demanded repayment of Ptolemy XII's debts and appointed himself arbitrator between the siblings. Ptolemy's forces controlled the city and had every interest in keeping Cleopatra away from him.",
        "She got in anyway. **Plutarch** says she was carried through the lines in a bed-sack by a single trusted servant and delivered to Caesar's quarters. That she reached him secretly is **Probable**; the rolled-carpet image everyone pictures is a later embellishment, classified **Traditional / Legendary**.",
        "The point of the story is usually missed. It is not about seduction. It is about correctly identifying that the throne of Egypt would be decided by one Roman in one room, and being physically willing to risk death to be in that room first. Her brother's advisors, with an army and a city, could not manage it."],
      key: "Cleopatra was smuggled to Caesar past her brother's lines — a calculated political move, not merely a romantic one." },
    { title: "The Alexandrian War and the Library",
      text: ["Pothinus and Achillas besieged Caesar in the palace quarter through the winter of 48–47 BC. Heavily outnumbered, Caesar held on until relief arrived overland; Ptolemy XIII drowned in the Nile fleeing the final battle, and Cleopatra was installed on the throne with the next brother, Ptolemy XIV.",
        "During the fighting Caesar fired ships in the harbour and the blaze spread ashore. Ancient accounts differ sharply on what burned. The likeliest reading is that warehoused scrolls near the docks were destroyed rather than the **Great Library** itself, which is attested as functioning afterwards. The Library declined over centuries through funding cuts, expulsions of scholars and later damage; the single catastrophic fire is a simplification. Classified **Contested**.",
        "The siege was fought street by street in the richest quarter of the richest city in the Mediterranean, with Caesar's few thousand men holding the palace, the theatre and a stretch of waterfront against a city that did not want them. Both sides cut the aqueducts and poisoned wells; Caesar's men dug for fresh water on the shore and found it. He nearly drowned swimming from a collapsing mole, and Suetonius says he kept his notebooks above the water with one hand.",
        "Relief came overland from Syria under **Mithridates of Pergamon**, with Jewish and Nabataean troops whose commander, Antipater, earned a Roman favour that his son Herod would eventually spend. Ptolemy XIII went into the Nile in the rout and did not come out, and his gold armour was recovered and displayed so that nobody could claim he had escaped."],
      key: "The Alexandrian War put Cleopatra on the throne; the Library's destruction in the fire is contested and likely overstated." },
    { title: "What the relationship actually was",
      text: ["There is one detail that keeps the human part of it from disappearing entirely into strategy. Caesar named a legion for her — and Cleopatra, who had every reason to be careful, named her son after the most dangerous name available rather than a safe dynastic one. Neither act was required by any calculation.",
        "Caesar stayed in Egypt for months he could not spare, with a civil war unfinished — a Nile journey with Cleopatra is reported, though its scale is disputed. She bore a son she named **Caesarion** and publicly claimed was his: **Probable**, and politically explosive, since Caesar's Roman heir was a grand-nephew.",
        "She came to Rome in 46 BC and stayed in Caesar's villa across the Tiber while he placed a gilded statue of her in the temple of Venus Genetrix — an extraordinary act, associating a living foreign queen with the divine ancestress of his own family. Romans found it scandalous, and Cicero, who met her, disliked her intensely.",
        "Read it as two rulers using each other, and both getting what they came for. He got Egypt's debts serviced, its grain, and a kingdom pacified in his interest. She got a throne, a Roman guarantee, and a son with a claim to the most powerful name in the world. That is not a lesser story than the romance. It is a considerably more dangerous one, and after the Ides of March it left her holding a child whose existence Caesar's heir could never tolerate."],
      key: "Caesar and Cleopatra's partnership served both: he secured Egypt's wealth and loyalty, she secured her throne and a son named for him." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why did Ptolemy XIII's court have Pompey killed?", options: ["He had attacked Egypt", "To please Caesar, on the calculation that dead men do not bite", "Religious obligation", "He owed them money"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It was a cynical calculation that backfired completely, drawing Caesar personally into Egypt's succession war." },
    { q: "How does the app treat the story of the Library burning down in Caesar's fire?", options: ["Established fact", "Contested — likely dockside scrolls, with the Library attested afterwards", "Traditional / Legendary — it never happened", "Unknown"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Something burned, but the single-catastrophe version does not fit the evidence for the Library's later operation." },
    { q: "What did each side gain from the Caesar–Cleopatra partnership?", options: ["Nothing political", "He gained Egypt's wealth and loyalty; she gained a throne, a guarantee and a son with Caesar's name", "She gained Roman citizenship", "He gained a claim to the Egyptian throne"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Reading it as mutual statecraft explains the decisions better than the romance narrative does." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "egy-end", set: "ptolemaic-egypt", act: "The Last Queen", title: "The Last Pharaoh", era: "44 – 30 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "Three centuries of Ptolemies and three thousand years of pharaohs end in a fortnight in Alexandria.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Antony",
      text: ["After Caesar's assassination, Cleopatra backed the winning side carefully and slowly. Summoned to **Tarsus** in 41 BC to explain Egypt's conduct, she arrived on a gilded barge dressed as **Aphrodite** — political theatre that Plutarch described at length and Shakespeare later lifted almost intact.",
        "The alliance with **Mark Antony** was transactional and also, by every account, personal. He needed Egyptian money and grain for a Parthian campaign; she needed a Roman protector and territory. She got more of the latter than any Ptolemy for a century: at the **Donations of Alexandria** in 34 BC, Antony distributed Roman-claimed eastern lands to their children and publicly confirmed **Caesarion** as Caesar's son.",
        "For Octavian, that last part was intolerable. His entire claim rested on being Caesar's heir.",
        "The middle years are usually skipped and they are the ones that explain the end. Antony spent the winter of 41 BC in Alexandria and then went back to Italy and married **Octavia**, Octavian's sister, in a treaty marriage that held the Roman world together for six years. Cleopatra, who had borne him twins in the meantime, raised them alone and ran Egypt.",
        "When he came back to her in 37 BC it was because he needed Egypt for the **Parthian** campaign, and that campaign was a disaster — perhaps a third of the army lost on the retreat through Armenia, in weather, without a battle he could name. He came out of it dependent on Egyptian money in a way he had not been before, and he never recovered the standing the failure cost him.",
        "Then he did the thing that could not be walked back. He divorced Octavia — in Rome, formally, by letter, ordering her out of his house — and staged the Donations in a Greek city, dressed as Dionysus, distributing Roman provinces to his children by a foreign queen."],
      key: "Cleopatra's alliance with Antony brought territorial expansion and, at the Donations of 34 BC, public confirmation of Caesarion." },
    { title: "Actium",
      text: ["Octavian's material was handed to him. He obtained, or claimed to obtain, Antony's will from the Vestal Virgins and read it aloud in the Senate: Antony wished to be buried in Alexandria, beside Cleopatra, rather than in Rome. Whether the document was genuine is **Contested** — seizing a will from the Vestals was itself a scandal, and it said exactly what Octavian needed it to say — but it did the work. A Roman who would not be buried in Rome had chosen a side.",
        "Octavian's response was a propaganda campaign of remarkable effectiveness, culminating in a war declared not on Antony but on **Cleopatra** — a foreign queen — so that no Roman need be seen fighting a Roman.",
        "The naval battle at **Actium** in 31 BC and the campaign around it are covered in the Wars tab. The outcome: the fleet broken, allies defecting, and Alexandria taken in August 30 BC. **Antony** stabbed himself on a false report of Cleopatra's death and died in her presence."],
      key: "Rome declared war on Cleopatra rather than Antony; Actium in 31 BC and the fall of Alexandria in 30 BC ended their cause." },
    { title: "How she died",
      text: ["She had eight days between Antony's death and her own, spent negotiating with Octavian for her children, and she used them to establish what he intended. Plutarch says a friendly Roman officer told her plainly that she was to be sent ahead to Rome in three days. She asked to visit Antony's tomb, bathed, dined, and dressed in full royal regalia.",
        "Cleopatra survived Antony by days and died at 39. Octavian intended to display her in his triumph, and she refused to be displayed.",
        "The **asp** hidden in a basket of figs is the famous version and is **Traditional / Legendary**. Plutarch, who tells it, states plainly that no one knows the truth, and mentions marks on her arm and a poison she may have prepared and tested in advance. A concealed venomous snake reliably killing one specific person on cue is not how snakes behave; a prepared poison fits the evidence at least as well.",
        "Her two surviving children by Antony were paraded in Octavian's triumph and then raised in Rome by **Octavia** — Antony's discarded Roman wife — which is among the stranger acts of decency in the whole story. Her daughter **Cleopatra Selene** later became queen of Mauretania."],
      key: "Cleopatra died in 30 BC rather than be paraded in a triumph; Plutarch himself says the asp story cannot be verified." },
    { title: "Egypt becomes property",
      text: ["Octavian is said to have had Caesarion lured back from the road to the Red Sea with a promise of the throne, on the advice of a tutor; the philosopher Arius supplied the justification with a line about it being no good thing to have too many Caesars. Antony's eldest son by Fulvia was killed at the same time. The children who were spared were the ones with no claim to anything.",
        "**Caesarion**, aged seventeen, was hunted down and killed. With him ended the Ptolemaic dynasty and, in a real sense, the institution of the pharaoh.",
        "Egypt was not made an ordinary province. Because it fed Rome, and whoever held it could starve the capital, **Augustus** took it as his personal possession, governed by a prefect of equestrian rank, and barred senators from entering without permission. The richest kingdom in the Mediterranean became the private granary of one household.",
        "Cleopatra's afterlife belonged to her enemies. Octavian's wartime propaganda — the foreign temptress who unmanned a Roman hero — was so effective that it remains the default image two thousand years later, reinforced by Roman poets writing under his patronage. Keep that in mind every time this app hands you a story that a winner wrote."],
      key: "Caesarion's death ended the pharaohs; Augustus made Egypt his personal possession, closed to senators." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What does Plutarch himself say about the asp?", options: ["He confirms it as certain", "He states that no one knows the truth and mentions alternatives", "He never mentions it", "He blames Octavian"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The source of the famous story is openly uncertain — a good demonstration of why classification matters." },
    { q: "Why did Augustus govern Egypt as personal property?", options: ["Religious custom", "Whoever controlled Egypt's grain could starve Rome", "It was too small for a governor", "The Senate refused it"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Keeping it out of senatorial hands removed the possibility of a rival using Egypt as a base against him." },
  ],
},
);
